description: Proposed: the corpus tests behaviors, one dense file per behavior, and it grows where issues are filed. The review rounds left about 1,500 files under `corpus/review*/`, most of them one expression; this keeps that from recurring.
 
 The PR from `corpus-conventions` carries it:
 
 1. `corpus/README.md`: the rules, which layer tests what, and a topic tree (`lang/numbers`, `lang/fns`, `lang/types`, ..., `targets/llvm`, `suites`, `apps`, `results`) to migrate to. Every directory gets a README saying what its topic covers and which check runs it; the 26 that lacked one have it now.
 2. The topics are git-issue labels, `corpus:<topic>`, and a label may go below a topic for clarity (`corpus:lang/types/protocols`). `checks/corpus-budget.tsv` gives each of today's directories an exact base and the topics it serves; its ceiling is that base plus 2 files and 16 KB for each live issue, new or existing, carrying one of its labels; an untagged child issue takes its nearest tagged ancestor's topics. An issue labelled `corpus-budget:<files>/<KB>` (e.g. `corpus-budget:6/64`) grants that instead of the default, for work that needs a library test, its fixtures and a re-freeze under one issue; the label needs a `corpus:` topic on the issue or an ancestor. Closing an issue takes its room away, and `checks/corpus-lint.sh --settle` makes what was actually added the new base. `checks/corpus-lint.sh` enforces it (row P0.2, pre-commit). Raising a base by hand needs an `Issue:` trailer (commit-msg). `review*/` only shrinks.
 
 I tagged the existing issues: 27 of 60 fit a topic, `lang/types` leading with 7, which is why it is a topic, and the open ones among them are the first room the budget grants. The other 33 are compile time, process and gate work, which grow no tests.
 
 Nothing moves yet; each directory moves in its own PR.
 
 Questions for you:
 - Does the topic tree work for you, or would you cut it differently?
 - You took AGENTS.md out when the working notes left the tree. Should a short root AGENTS.md point at the README's Checks section and `corpus/README.md`, or should agent guidance stay out of the tree?

## Comments
[2026-10-19T02:44:19Z] Matt Parrett: Read #53 at its head and the budget rules. Answers to the two questions, then one change I want before it merges.
 
 **Topic tree.** It works. The one cut I would make: `records` as a top-level topic beside `lang/types` splits the same mechanism (D122 tags records as maps on the wasm lane, and the llvm type model is D218), so I would put it at `lang/types/records` and leave the top level for things that are not language features.
 
 **Root AGENTS.md.** Keep agent guidance out of the tree. The README's Checks section and `corpus/README.md` are what an agent should read, and a root file that only points at them is a third place to keep current. If a tool needs a fixed entry point, a one-line pointer in the README's first section does the same job.
 
 **The ceiling.** Two files and 16 KB per live issue is right for a behavior fix, and wrong for the next numeric-tower pieces: a piece with a native oracle wants the library test, the fixture set the oracle check reads, and the jank re-freeze, in one PR under one issue. That is three to five files, mostly in `corpus/intrinsics/` and `corpus/jank/`. Two options, either is fine with me: a per-issue override field in the issue (`corpus_budget: 6 files 64 KB`) that the lint reads, or a larger default for `intrinsics/` and `jank/` in `corpus-budget.tsv`. I prefer the override, since it keeps the default honest and makes the exception visible on the issue that asked for it.
 
 With that settled I will review #53 for merge.
[2026-10-19T03:18:39Z] Norman Nunley, Jr: I took the override, and #53 has it now: an issue labelled `corpus-budget:6/64` grants 6 files and 64 KB instead of the default 2 and 16. git-issue has no custom fields, so it is a label, which shows on the issue the same way. A malformed one fails the lint, and so does one on an issue with no `corpus:` topic, since `--labels` replaces the whole list.
 
 On `records`: I meant results, not defrecord. That topic held gate logs, census tables and `*-results.tsv`, so the name was the problem. It is `results` now, and defrecord stays under `lang/types` with D122 and D218. If you want records called out, `corpus:lang/types/records` already works as a label below `lang/types`.
 
 No root AGENTS.md. The README already points at `corpus/README.md` from its corpus bullet.
